# %% [markdown]
# # EXPERIMENT v9 -- v8 + controlled-return supervision off the data (checkpoints_v9)
#
# v8 with ONE change, in the collocation loss: besides the data points, the
# field is supervised at states slightly OFF the data, with a target that
# returns to the data at a controlled rate:
#
#     z' = z + delta                       (z a data state, |delta| ~ 0.005..0.3)
#     v_target(z') = v_true(z) - K * delta,   K_k = RETURN_KAPPA * s_g(k)(z)
#
# s_g = the parent's speed in rate group g (1/s, normalized units), so the
# pull-back rate of every variable is tied to how fast its own group moves at
# that point -- the field off the data becomes "move like the nearby data,
# and drift back to it", with no time scale faster than the data's own.
# Why (pretrain_compare.py, g4's vs v8's pretrained field): the data only
# define the field ON the trajectories; two fields equally accurate there
# (v8's even better: ignition 1.8 % vs 3.9 %) differed just off it -- v8's had a
# spot ~50x faster than its typical rate at the ignition front, where w8
# windows drifted (max |z - z_true| ~ 1.9) and needed > 1000 solver steps, and
# failed windows give no gradient.  The TRUE off-data field is no answer: it is
# 4-230x faster than on the data and was measured unfittable and harmful
# (augment_collocation.py header).  So the off-data field is SPECIFIED: a
# smooth, controlled return (the slow-manifold idea of reduced chemistry).
# ONLY THE FAST VARIABLES are perturbed (RETURN_VARIABLES "fast": every species
# except T and MAJOR_SPECIES).  T and the majors say WHICH trajectory a state is
# on; a different value there is another valid operating point, not an error.
# Measured (8 local cases, 8k pretraining steps, best checkpoint, median error
# on the data): perturbing ALL variables pulls validation cases towards the
# nearest training case -- train 15.2 % / val 22.7 % vs 8.5 % / 12.6 % without
# off-data points; fast variables only: 6.2 % / 12.2 %.
# Settings: RETURN_FRACTION (share of collocation points, 0 = v8), RETURN_KAPPA,
# RETURN_SIGMA_MIN / MAX, RETURN_PER_POINT.  The points are generated once at
# start-up (no Cantera calls) for every case; train / val use their own cases.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# v8 header:
# # EXPERIMENT v8 -- v7 + exact element (and mass) conservation (checkpoints_v8)
#
# v7 with ONE change: the velocity the solver integrates is projected, at every
# evaluation, onto the set of velocities that conserve the elements C, H, O
# (every element present in the learned species):
#
#     sum_k M_ke dY_k/dt = 0     for every element e,  all learned species k
#
# M_ke = mass of element e per unit mass of species k.  In the model's
# variables dY_k/dt = (Y_k + eps) ln10 s_k dz_k/dt, so the constraint is
# LINEAR in the velocity, A(z) v = 0, and the projection is the orthogonal one
# in z-space:  v <- v - A^T (A A^T)^-1 A v  (3x3 solve per state).
#   * total mass follows: every learned species is made of C, H, O only and
#     the inerts (N2, AR) are constant, so sum_k dY_k/dt = 0 as well;
#   * the correction on species k is proportional to (Y_k + eps) * (its element
#     content): it lands on the species that carry the element mass (H2, H2O,
#     CO, CO2, CH4, O2); trace species are in the constraint but are barely
#     moved -- they cannot carry an element imbalance;
#   * Cantera's true velocity satisfies A v = 0, so at the true states the
#     projection can only REDUCE the velocity error (orthogonal projection onto
#     a subspace that contains the target);
#   * the collocation loss stays on the network's raw rate / direction outputs
#     (as in v7): pretraining is v7's; the trajectory stages train through the
#     projection.  ELEMENT_CONSERVATION false = v7's model (the buffers are
#     still saved, the projection is off).
#   * remaining drift = solver error only (the conserved sums are linear in Y,
#     the state is log Y), i.e. ~ the solver tolerance.
# Why (error_map.py on checkpoints_v7_steam_g4): the tail error is inherited
# -- restarted from the true tail start the end-T error is 0.4 K, and the true
# equilibrium is a fixed point of the learned field -- while the element
# composition drifts by H 10 %, C 5.8 %, O 4.4 % (mean, end of rollout), an
# error the dynamics can never undo.
# Default RATE_GROUPS is v7's best run: "T;CH4,O2;H2O" (4 groups).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# v7 header:
# # EXPERIMENT v7 -- grouped rates (checkpoints_v7)
#
# v4 with ONE change in the model: the single shared log-rate is replaced by
# one shared log-rate PER GROUP of variables (RATE_GROUPS, default
# "T;CH4,O2": group 1 = T, group 2 = CH4 + O2, group 3 = every other
# variable).  Within a group the model is exactly v4's factorization,
#     dz_k/dt = exp(r_g(z,P)) * h_k(z,P)      for k in group g,
# each r_g bounded by that group's own data range, direction targets
# normalized by the GROUP speed.
# Why (next_step_diagnostic.py on the v4 checkpoints): with one shared rate,
# the rate during induction is set by the fast radicals, so T, CH4 and O2 are
# tiny components of the direction vector (|h| ~ 3e-2, 2e-3, 1e-3) and their
# errors relative to their OWN rate were 17 / 133 / 240 % median in induction
# already after pretraining (w250: 43 / 192 / 724 %).  In their own group
# they are O(1) components.  A few groups only, unlike v5/v6 (a rate per
# variable), whose rollouts were unstable or did not ignite.
# Also (for feeds with steam + N2): INERT_SPECIES (default N2, AR) are taken
# out of the learned state and carried per case from the initial state,
# instead of v4's rule (drop only channels constant across ALL data, restore
# them from case 0).
# Everything else (data, losses, curriculum, flags) is v4.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# v4 header (unchanged below):
# # EXPERIMENT v4 -- run 2 (checkpoints_v4)
#
# Same model, data handling and curriculum w8 ... w250 as v3
# (train_node_rate_field_v3.py; run 2 reuses run 1's pretraining and w8-w250
# checkpoints, copied into checkpoints_v4).  What changed:
#   * ONE full-trajectory stage from the w250 model: squared-error loss + majors
#     mass-fraction term, no start-state noise, 32 trajectories per step,
#     lr 3e-5 -> 3e-6, 200 epochs (at batch_scale 4) -- replaces run 1's
#     "full" (Huber, noise) and the abandoned "full2" continuation;
#   * every setting is a command-line flag (section 0), env vars still work;
#   * final evaluation = continuous rollout only (--eval_reset for the reset
#     rollout as a diagnostic); results named after the last stage;
#   * --stop_after pretrain: pretraining only + derivative-accuracy report
#     (network-size check); --noise_at_t0 (default true = as run in v3).
#
# # Autonomous Neural ODE for GRI-Mech partial oxidation -- corrected baseline
#
# Replacement for `train_multi_shooting_batched.py`.
#
# The model is, and stays, a genuine NODE:
#
#     dz/dt = F(z, P)          z = normalized [T, log10(Y_i)], P = pressure
#
# No time input, no latent space, no state->state mapping.  Time only ever
# enters as the *integration variable*, and as the per-window duration that
# converts d/dt into d/dtau for a batched solve (a change of variable, not a
# network input).
#
# WHAT WAS WRONG BEFORE (each item is measured -- see
# NODE_DIAGNOSIS_AND_FIXES.md for the numbers and the reasoning):
#
# 1. EVALUATION BUG.  `reset_rollout` / `continuous_rollout` integrated on a
#    UNIFORM tau grid but compared/plotted against the NON-UNIFORM physical
#    sample times.  A *perfect* model scored ~510 K T-RMSE on the continuous
#    rollout and ~5.7 K on the w=20 reset rollout with that code.  Every
#    rollout here is evaluated at the true sample times.
#
# 2. dz/dtau CLAMP.  Clamping dz/dtau to +-2 makes the fastest 15% of w=20
#    windows unrepresentable (they need up to 1.2e6) AND kills the gradient
#    exactly there (clamp is flat).  That is the direct cause of accuracy
#    degrading monotonically with window length.  Removed; stability is
#    obtained instead from a *physically* bounded rate (see RateField).
#
# 3. DERIVATIVE PARAMETERIZATION.  Predicting symlog10(dz/dt) with a Tanh
#    head and decoding with 10**(.) makes the state derivative exponentially
#    sensitive to the network output: the reported pretrain MSE of 1.1e-2
#    corresponds to typical derivative errors of a FACTOR ~5.  Replaced by a
#    factorization
#           dz/dt = exp(r(z,P)) * h(z,P),
#    r = bounded scalar log-rate (the local chemical time scale, in nats),
#    h = linear O(1) direction vector.  Only one scalar is exponential, it is
#    bounded by the data's own rate range, and the natural training signal is
#    "relative speed error + direction error", which is exactly the error
#    model that matters for trajectory accuracy.
#
# 4. DERIVATIVE LOSS AT PREDICTED STATES.  The old code compared
#    f(z_predicted) against dz/dt_true(z_true) -- two different points.  Here
#    the derivative (collocation) loss is evaluated at the TRUE states, which
#    is what the exact Cantera derivatives actually supervise.
#
# 5. WINDOW RESAMPLING.  The old code linearly interpolated every window onto
#    a uniform tau grid, i.e. trained on fabricated targets (up to 0.12 in
#    scaled units == 0.6 decades in ignition windows) and threw away the real
#    samples.  Here the solver is given each window's true taus, and batching
#    is done with a per-sample adaptive stepper (batched_ode.py), which is
#    exact and ~50x cheaper than forcing one shared adaptive grid on windows
#    whose fast features sit at different places in tau.
#
# 6. LEARNING-RATE SCHEDULE.  Stages 2/3 started at 1e-5 / 2e-6 and spent
#    ~700 epochs at the 1e-7 floor: those stages could not learn.  Each stage
#    here has its own cosine schedule from a real LR.
#
# 7. DEAD CHANNELS.  AR and N2 are identically zero in this dataset; their
#    scaled derivative target was -1, i.e. the Tanh head was asked to
#    saturate.  Constant channels are dropped from the state and restored on
#    output.
#
# Author note: everything below is the data-driven baseline only.  Mass /
# element / energy conservation hooks are marked "PHYSICS HOOK" but are NOT
# active -- get this baseline converged first.

